# =============================================================================
# TEST 126 — DENSE DIRECTIONAL EXISTENCE ATLAS
# AkbasCore — New Paradigm / Phase I
#
# PRIMARY QUESTION
# =============================================================================
#
# Does a dense, angle-controlled directional neighborhood around the locked
# semantic compass contain intervention directions that produce BETTER
# terminal semantic transport than the compass itself?
#
#                       EXISTS q :
#
#             D19(q) > D19(v18)  ?
#
# =============================================================================
#
# IMPORTANT:
#
# This is NOT a navigator test.
# This is NOT a runtime sensor test.
# This is NOT DRA.
#
# It is an ORACLE / SYSTEM-IDENTIFICATION experiment.
#
# We first establish whether a better route exists at all.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MODEL
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct
# BF16
# 28 layers
# hidden = 3584
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INTERVENTION
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source layer        : L18 output / entering L19
# Position            : last token only
# Terminal endpoint   : L19 output
# Reference direction : locked ABOVE vector v18
# Magnitude           : equal relative norm = 0.025
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DIRECTION ATLAS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1000 candidate directions:
#
#   2° shell  : 200
#   5° shell  : 200
#  10° shell  : 200
#  20° shell  : 150
#  35° shell  : 150
#  global     : 100
#
# Every seed-shell candidate is constructed EXACTLY at its assigned angle:
#
#   q = cos(theta) v18 + sin(theta) u
#
# with:
#
#   u ⟂ v18
#   ||u|| = 1
#
# Therefore candidates within the same shell have equal semantic distance
# from the seed by construction.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRIMARY ENDPOINT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# For intervention direction q:
#
#   delta19(q) = h19(q) - h19(BASE)
#
#   D19(q) = cos(delta19(q), v19)
#
# Reference:
#
#   D19(seed) = cos(delta19(v18), v19)
#
# Candidate advantage:
#
#   Delta(q) = D19(q) - D19(seed)
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRIMARY PHASE-I EVIDENCE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. Is the best direction in each prompt better than seed?
# 2. How large is the improvement?
# 3. Does the improvement already exist inside a FIXED ANGLE shell?
# 4. What fraction of same-angle candidates beat seed?
# 5. How much directional dispersion exists after angle is held fixed?
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMPORTANT ANTI-OVERCLAIM RULE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# max over 1000 candidates is an ORACLE maximum and contains winner's-curse /
# multiple-search optimism.
#
# Therefore:
#
#   max(q) > seed
#
# alone is NOT treated as proof.
#
# We also report:
#
#   - fraction of candidates beating seed
#   - shell mean vs seed
#   - top-decile mean vs seed
#   - within-shell dispersion
#   - prompt consistency
#
# TEST127, using NEW prompts, will determine whether discovered opportunities
# are context-dependent and whether any global candidate generalizes.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO:
# controller
# zeta
# old brake
# Mercek
# local probe sensor
# PCA
# Jacobian claim
# gradient
# weight update
# KV modification
# generation / behavioral endpoint
#
# =============================================================================


